{"kind":"request","text":"Make tracked artifacts—not the work queue—the primary human-facing product surface. Gitseq already projects every artifact's path, commit, current/stale/retired state, provenance, and tracking warnings, but the current browser shows only stale or retired artifacts inside the miscellaneous `Other attention` list. Current artifacts have no catalog, no file preview, and no direct `why does this need review?` path. This hides the end result normal readers care about and leaves the README's central promise without a usable interface.\n\nAdd a top-level Artifacts view alongside Work and Activity, and make it the initial human-facing view while preserving the reader's most recent explicit view choice. The view is an inventory of every effective tracked artifact, grouped by its exact stable path and showing the current live generation first. Use plain states: `Current`, `Needs review` for projection-stale, `Retired`, `Cannot detect changes` for unable-to-flare, and `Replacement not recorded` for succession gaps. Never label a stale artifact `wrong`: a flare means that a premise moved and the artifact must be re-checked. Retired history is hidden behind the shared hidden-history disclosure from #2786 rather than mixed into current outputs.\n\nSelecting an artifact opens a centered inspector whose first screen answers four questions without exposing event mechanics: what is it; what exact commit does it name; is it still current; and, if not, what changed. For `Needs review`, show the shortest causal path from the artifact through its live provenance to each retired ancestor, the effective supersession that replaced it, actor and time, with controls to inspect the old premise, its replacement, and the complete dependency chain. Keep `retired`, `stale`, `describes a superseded world`, `unable to flare`, and `succession not recorded` distinct in the detailed view. Reuse the durable Inspect interaction from #2728 rather than inventing a second event inspector.\n\nAdd a bounded read-only repository tree and blob API at an explicitly selected commit, using the existing guarded Git store primitives. When an artifact path resolves to a file or directory at its commit, the inspector previews that committed content and the Artifacts view can browse its containing tree. Render text and Markdown safely, identify binary/oversize content without reading it into memory, validate commit and path input, prevent traversal, cap directory and blob responses with skipped/size information, and never read working-tree bytes. If an artifact's free-form path does not resolve to a repository object, keep it as a valid logical artifact with provenance and no file preview; do not silently treat every label as a filename.\n\nProvide a Files mode for browsing committed repository objects and overlay tracked-artifact state wherever an exact path has one. Untracked files are neutrally `Not tracked`, never presumed current or defective; do not imply that gitseq automatically understands imports, file paths, or every repository object. `.` represents the committed repository snapshot. Coordinate this surface with #2759's Repository modal: repository status, remotes, branches, and railway remain repository context, while the artifact catalog and its file preview remain a main product view. Work stays the operational view for requests and review.\n\nInclude search and filters for state, path, actor, and commit; current/needs-review totals; deterministic ordering; direct URLs/routes that reopen the selected artifact; empty, reset, missing-object, rewritten-branch, SHA-1/SHA-256, and very large repository behavior; keyboard and screen-reader navigation; focused service and UI tests; and committed-embed verification. Editing files, automatically repairing artifacts, guessing dependencies, and rendering arbitrary active content are out of scope.","body":{"branch":"request/ui-artifact-browser","conditions":"A top-level Artifacts view lists current tracked outputs as well as needs-review and tracking-problem states, with retired history behind #2786; plain language never equates stale with wrong; selecting an artifact shows path, exact commit, state, shortest causal explanation, retired premise, effective replacement, actor/time, and links to shared inspection; bounded read-only commit tree/blob endpoints safely preview resolvable repository-backed artifacts while logical paths remain valid without preview; Files mode overlays exact-path tracking states and labels other objects neutrally untracked; `.` is handled as a repository snapshot; state/path/actor/commit search and filters, stable routes, caps, traversal/binary/oversize/missing/reset/object-format/accessibility cases are tested; #2728, #2759, and #2786 interactions are coherent; UI unit/build/embed-drift and repository gates pass.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}